The World Health Organization made a standardized grading scale to measure the severity of bleeding.

Grade 0
No bleeding;

Grade 1
Petechial bleeding;

Grade 2
Mild blood loss (clinically significant);

Grade 3
Gross blood loss, requires transfusion (severe);

Grade 4
Debilitating blood loss, retinal or cerebral associated with fatality

@b.nota - one thing you've not thought about - aphids are always with us, the only thing that prevents a plant from getting reinfested within an hour is use of systemic insecticide, and obviously, you can't use that. So if you bought the larvae, and they managed to clear the infestation you have now before they disappear, there's a whole army of aphids waiting in the wings to attack your basil ongoing. If you're gentle and don't break stems, you can rub them off the plant with your fingers, bit messy, but effective

